  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Find the unknown constants if $P(x)=ax^2+b(x+1)^2+c(x+2)^2$ and $Q(x)=-x^2+3x$ have the same graph.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=-\\dfrac{13}{4},\\quad b=3,\\quad c=-\\dfrac34$.",
      "worked": "The same graph means $P(x)=Q(x)$ for all $x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=ax^2+b(x^2+2x+1)+c(x^2+4x+4)\\\\P(x)&=(a+b+c)x^2+(2b+4c)x+b+4c.\n\\end{align*}$$\nEquating coefficients gives\n$$a+b+c=-1,\\qquad 2b+4c=3,\\qquad b+4c=0.$$\nSubtracting the constant-term equation from the linear-coefficient equation gives\n$$\\begin{align*}b&=3\\\\3+4c&=0\\\\c&=-\\frac34\\\\a&=-1-b-c\\\\a&=-1-3+\\frac34\\\\a&=-\\frac{13}{4}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
